{"kind":"artifact","text":"Implementation report for the extracted onboarding-identity core at exact head 33843691460da55c7c20fdca62db4e825bef0b86 on request/onboarding-identity-core (pushed; fast-forwards from main c19f4eb9). This replaces the withdrawn candidate 68bd30a5, which stood at the defective 358a46d4, and answers both defects codex found there.\n\nWHAT IS HERE. host/identity, in the host layer: the fixed vocabulary (gitseq/identity-witness@0, gitseq/identity-anchor@0, gitseq/identity-revoke@0), GitHub-login witnessing under a witness key declared in the log by the initializing key, delegation for a second device or an agent credential, revocation, and a two-axis anchored/unanchored resolution folded from the log alone with no clock and no network. It gates no append: an unauthorized, unparseable, malformed or over-claiming record resolves to nothing and leaves the previous answer standing.\n\nREPAIR 1, SCOPE. The exported SelfSigned vouching value could not be produced. Witnessing was the only ground assignment in resolve.go, and the delegation branch's min(SelfSigned, parent.vouching) had no base case of its own, so no log could resolve a key to it. The extracted scope names only GitHub. The public constant and its String case are removed; a delegation now inherits parent.vouching outright, which is what the code already computed. The self-signed rung — where the identity's own key signs, which is where Nostr and a published forge signing key would sit — is stated as deferred in the package doc and in docs/reference/architecture.md, and the axis stays ordered so adding it later is one more value plus the reduction rule the verification axis already follows. This head therefore claims one implemented vouching rung, witnessed, and no other.\n\nREPAIR 2, SECURITY. GitHub.Check promised the caller's bearer token never reaches an error and had two seams where it did. A provider writes its own HTTP/1.1 reason phrase and this package returned response.Status verbatim, so a provider echoing what it was sent put the credential into an error and from there into a log. The HTTP client is the deployment's to set, so a round-tripper reporting the request it was handed published the Authorization header through the wrapped transport error. Both are closed by a rule with no exceptions rather than by care at each return: no byte of provider- or transport-controlled text reaches an error from here. A refusal reports statusText(code) — the numeric code with Go's own reason phrase, built locally from the number and never read off the status line. A transport failure and a JSON decode failure report fixed words of this package's own. Only the caller's own cancellation still surfaces, via ctx.Err(), because that answer belongs to the caller's context and not to the transport. No substring redaction anywhere, and no withoutToken helper exists: redaction removes only the spelling it goes looking for, and the party echoing the credential chooses the spelling.\n\nTESTS ACTUALLY RUN, at this exact head. Three adversarial tests in host/identity/identity_test.go: a raw-socket server writing \"401 Unauthorized-\u003ctoken\u003e\" as the reason phrase, which httptest cannot produce because its server composes the phrase from Go's own table; a transport echoing \"Authorization: Bearer \u003ctoken\u003e\" in its error; and a cancelled-context case asserting errors.Is(err, context.Canceled) still holds. FAIL-FIRST PROOF: with both guards reverted to response.Status and the wrapped transport error, all three fail, and the first two fail printing the literal token — \"github user lookup: 401 Unauthorized-gho_reason_phrase_secret\" and \"github user lookup: Get \\\"https://provider.invalid/user\\\": dial refused while sending Authorization: Bearer gho_transport_secret\". Restoring the guards turns them green. The assertions are written from the desired behaviour, not transcribed from output.\n\nGATES, all at 33843691. gofmt -l clean; go build ./... clean; go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... green across all 28 packages including internal/docset, the uncached citation gate that checks the architecture edits still resolve, and host/identity; go test -race -count=1 ./... green across the same set, exit 0.\n\nCONDITIONS. The public surface is the exported Go API axis under layer 4 and it is narrower than the withdrawn candidate by exactly one constant, with no behaviour any log could reach removed with it. Witness custody remains the deployment's under the supported single-operator posture; authenticated shared-host support stays deferred; the two-axis display surface belongs to the application. This package authenticates nobody and authorizes nothing.","body":{"commit":"33843691460da55c7c20fdca62db4e825bef0b86","path":"host"}}